@Crazyderk6989Agreed, and I’ve had the same observations. For about 3 years I’ve been doing one 3-day water fast a month (sometimes 2 days water, 3rd day chicken broth, soups, plain probiotic yogurt and a kiwi). About a month and a half ago (when the whopper thing happened and I did a 3 day water fast to get it out of me), I started becoming super disciplined with only eating between 12pm and 8pm and fasting for 16 hours a day. I did my research for the best nourishing diet for women in perimenopause and removed all inflammatory foods from my diet and replaced them with anti-inflammatory foods. I searched for and bought a wide variety of natural proteins,  also added 3 Brazil nuts a day to my regiment, started drinking my home-made teas from what I grow in my gardens (current favourite being a mix of lavender and catnip…the leaves and flowers from both) etc…and my big belly is now gone and  I haven’t had a suicidal headache or migraine since the switch.

The gut has a HUGE impact on the brain and nervous system. HUGE.

Sometimes I wonder if the reason people keep torturing themselves is because of the affect (and effect) their poor diet has on their brains. When a brain is impaired it doesn’t always know it’s impaired and keeps making the same self destructive choices over and over again. It’s tragic. I think desensitization has something to do with it too but I somehow missed that bus. Phew.
